fields.description:
Symfony Console commands currently obtain the plugin manager through:

{noformat}\core\plugin_manager::instance(){noformat}

[https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-85847|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-85847|smart-link] keeps this existing access pattern so that its scope remains focused on adding Symfony Console support.

This follow-up should assess whether {{core\plugin_manager}} should also be exposed as a DI entry, allowing consumers such as Symfony commands to receive it through constructor injection.

Initial implementation may include:

* a DI definition that resolves {{core\plugin_manager}} through its existing {{instance()}} method;
* constructor injection in the plugin-management Symfony commands;
* updating relevant command tests to override the DI entry with a mock plugin manager.

This issue should not remove or change the existing {{plugin_manager::instance()}} API unless there is a separately justified reason to do so. The aim is to make constructor injection available at appropriate boundaries, not to replace the plugin manager’s established lifecycle throughout Moodle.

h2. Background

[https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88176|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88176|smart-link] changed {{core_customfield\handler::create}} from returning a new instance by default to returning a singleton.

In 5.1, the base class explicitly allowed each handler to choose whether to use singleton caching. In 5.2, caching is now imposed on every subclass. Plugins that relied on fresh instances or stored state in their handlers can now potentially leak state between calls.

MDL-88176 was based on the premise that “every extending class” overrode {{create}} to cache a singleton. That is true in core, but not true generally. The [mod_cms|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms):] plugin’s {{cmsuserlist_handler}} [inherits the create method|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms/blob/5d1ba37d0cbd9a88404d725033a7bac4cb7f08c9/classes/customfield/cmsuserlist_handler.php#L32-L36)] and stores the active CMS in the handler, which its [datasource assigns|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms/blob/5d1ba37d0cbd9a88404d725033a7bac4cb7f08c9/classes/local/datasource/userlist.php#L67-L70).] after calling create.

h2. Replication

Add this script to your Moodle root in a 5.1 install and a 5.2 install:

{code:php}<?php

namespace {
    define('CLI_SCRIPT', true);
    require(__DIR__ . '/config.php');
}

namespace local_lifecycletest\customfield {
    use core_customfield\field_controller;
    final class example_handler extends \core_customfield\handler {
        public $value = '';

        public function get_configuration_context(): \context {
            return \context_system::instance();
        }

        public function get_configuration_url(): \moodle_url {
              return new \moodle_url('/');
        }

        public function get_instance_context(int $instanceid = 0): \context {
            return \context_system::instance();
        }

        public function can_configure(): bool {
            return true;
        }

        public function can_edit(field_controller $field, int $instanceid = 0): bool {
            return true;
        }

        public function can_view(field_controller $field, int $instanceid): bool {
            return true;
        }
  }
}

namespace {
    $first = \local_lifecycletest\customfield\example_handler::create();
    $first->value = 'first';

    $second = \local_lifecycletest\customfield\example_handler::create();
    $second->value = 'second';

    echo 'Same instance: ' . ($first === $second ? 'yes' : 'no') . PHP_EOL;
    echo 'First value: ' . $first->value . PHP_EOL;
}{code}

Run it in 5.1:

{noformat}$ php thing.php
Same instance: no
First value: first{noformat}

Run it in 5.2:

{noformat}$ php thing.php
Same instance: yes
First value: second{noformat}

h2. Proposed Fix

This is a backwards compatibility regression in a documented public API. The [Custom fields API docs|https://moodledev.io/docs/5.2/apis/core/customfields] state that “Some areas may choose to return a singleton here”, documenting singleton caching as _optional_. [Moodle's deprecation policy|https://moodledev.io/general/development/policies/deprecation#what-is-moodles-deprecation-policy] treats APIs expected to be called externally or overridden as public, while the [integration review process|https://moodledev.io/general/development/process/integration#integration-review-process] starts from the requirement that backwards compatibility must always be maintained.

So, there is no choice but to restore the pre-5.2 contract. The inherited {{create}} method must return a fresh handler instance, while individual handlers remain free to use singleton caching. The behaviour shipped in 5.2 is a regression and must be corrected rather than becoming the new public API.
